ROCR-Runtime (libhsakmt fmm.c and hsa-runtime flag.h), gfx803: stop the fmm
aperture allocator from handing a freed GPU virtual address (VA) straight
back to the next allocation, which corrupts MIOpen reduction kernels on
gfx803 (Polaris). A freed object parks with its BOs alive on a
length-bounded FIFO and is freed for real only inside a later free call,
while the GPU is busy. The hsa-runtime SimpleHeap fragment sub-allocator, a
second VA recycler above libhsakmt, defaults to off.

WHY
---
miopenReduceTensor (the dynamic CompositeKernel reduction path) on gfx803
intermittently returns wrong sums for a subset of output columns. ONNX
Runtime's ReductionOpTest.ReduceSum_apex_matrix_large shows it, and a
standalone 52-shape MIOpen-only harness reproduces it. The trigger is VA
recycling: with recycled input and output device addresses about 10 of 52
shapes come out wrong, and with fresh, never-recycled addresses 0 of 52 do.

VAs are recycled at two layers, and both have to be blocked:

  1. The hsa-runtime SimpleHeap fragment sub-allocator
     (core/util/simple_heap.h, used from core/driver/kfd/amd_kfd_driver.cpp)
     caches freed 2 MB blocks and serves the same VA to the next sub-2 MB
     allocation, entirely above libhsakmt. With only the fmm part of this
     patch and the fragment allocator on, the harness still fails 10 of 52
     shapes.
  2. The fmm aperture free-list in libhsakmt hands a freed range to the
     next allocation of the same size at once.

Delaying reuse is necessary and not sufficient. Tearing parked objects down
at a GPU-quiescent point leaves 5 to 7 of 52 shapes wrong. Never returning
VAs exhausts the VA space under real workloads: ReductionOpTest goes from
28.5 s to a 900 s timeout with over 400 failures. What clears the fault is
freeing the parked BO after a bounded window, inside a later free call,
while the GPU is busy. rocm6.4.4/patches/rocr/va-reuse-defer.patch holds
the full measurement record for the variants.

WHAT
----
- fmm.c, release path: __fmm_release() takes the aperture's fmm_mutex and
  calls __fmm_release_locked(), which holds the release body. Once an
  object's last userptr registration is dropped, __fmm_release_locked()
  parks the object instead of freeing it. A parked object keeps its BOs and
  stays in the aperture tree, so its VA stays reserved, and it is marked
  `deferred`; a second release of the same address is a no-op.
  fmm_defer_release() appends it to a per-aperture FIFO. When the FIFO holds
  more than FMM_REUSE_QUEUE_MAX (256) entries or FMM_REUSE_BYTES_MAX (1 GiB)
  of parked bytes, fmm_do_release() frees the oldest entries' BOs, returns
  their areas to the free-list and drops the objects. That happens inside
  the free call that pushed the FIFO over its bound, so the teardown runs
  while the GPU is busy.
- Three kinds of object are released at once instead of parked:
  * Scratch backing. fmm_release_scratch() loops until the scratch
    aperture's tree is empty, so a parked object would make it spin
    forever at process exit.
  * SVM-backed paged memory (is_svm_paged). It has no BO to keep alive, and
    its release has to munmap the pages and drop the SVM range reference,
    which fmm_do_release() does not do.
  * Objects in an mmap aperture. fmm_retire_stale_userptrs() releases a
    stale userptr registration through __fmm_release_locked() and needs the
    object out of the tree before its caller publishes a new object at that
    address. A parked registration would keep shadowing the new object,
    and the next GPU access through it faults.
  On gfx803 only the scratch case occurs. is_svm_paged needs the KFD SVM
  API, which gfx8 nodes do not report (SVMAPISupported), and
  init_svm_apertures() sets up mmap apertures only for a GPUVM limit of at
  least 2^47, while gfx8's GPUVM is 40-bit.
- flag.h: HSA_DISABLE_FRAGMENT_ALLOCATOR defaults to disabling the fragment
  sub-allocator. Set it to "0" to enable the allocator again.

COST: an O(1) FIFO push and pop on the free path. The number of BO-free
ioctls is unchanged, only delayed, and the VRAM that parked BOs hold is
bounded by FMM_REUSE_BYTES_MAX. Measured on ROCm 6.4.4: ONNX Runtime wall
time -0.41% against stock over six interleaved runs, inside noise.

Applies to: rocm-systems release/therock-10.2, commit
43fa692e568e65a1fb84f2d1c74415b042839f10. va-reuse-defer-mapping.patch and
va-reuse-defer-noremap.patch edit the park branch and fmm_do_release(), so
they apply after this patch.

VERIFIED ON REAL HARDWARE (RX 470 gfx803) on ROCm 6.4.4: the 52-shape
harness 0 of 52 wrong (stock 6 to 10 of 52); ONNX Runtime ReductionOpTest
suite 40 runs with no wrong sum and 2 page faults on kernel 6.19.10, and 169
of 170 runs clean (1 page fault) on kernel 7.1.7.

NOT YET RE-VERIFIED ON REAL HARDWARE on ROCm 10.2. To verify, run ONNX
Runtime's ReductionOpTest suite (gtest filter `*ReductionOpTest*`) several
times in an image built from the commit above. No wrong sums are expected;
ReduceSum_apex_matrix_large is the case that fails without this patch.

